1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,767
Imagine a math model facing a contest problem,

2
00:00:02,767 --> 00:00:05,733
but your budget gives you only fifty inference calls

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,033
How should you spend those fifty calls?

4
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:14,533
We used to make models think deeper, step by step

5
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:16,400
In 2025, researchers asked:

6
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:20,133
should test-time compute go into generating more answers,

7
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:22,467
or checking them more carefully?

8
00:00:24,033 --> 00:00:26,467
The first plan: search.

9
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:31,833
Generate fifty independent answers — fifty blocks, each one a fresh attempt

10
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:36,367
Then tally them up — the most frequent answer wins.

11
00:00:36,367 --> 00:00:39,433
One condition must hold: the model has to be right

12
00:00:39,433 --> 00:00:41,933
more than half the time for the trick to work

13
00:00:42,567 --> 00:00:44,800
Counting costs no extra inference

14
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:48,367
the majority answer wins, and that's plan A

15
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:52,733
The second plan: verification.

16
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:58,667
Generate five answers, then bring in a judge — the model itself — to review each one

17
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:05,100
Each answer is checked nine times — passes get a check, failures a cross

18
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:10,233
Five answers, nine checks each — that already uses up the whole budget

19
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,867
That's plan B: generate less, review more.

20
00:01:14,867 --> 00:01:18,967
Let the judge decide — review sounds like the smart move.

21
00:01:18,967 --> 00:01:21,133
Let's see if it is.

22
00:01:22,567 --> 00:01:27,200
Plan A spends everything on generating, plan B trades generation for checking

23
00:01:27,267 --> 00:01:29,267
and anything in between is possible

24
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:33,667
Three models with different skill levels share the same fifty-call budget

25
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:37,033
solid lines are search, dashed lines are verification

26
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,900
The result is consistent: search wins across the board

27
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,500
by roughly nine to twelve percentage points at the fifty-call mark

28
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,733
But keep one condition in mind: the judge here is the model itself

29
00:01:49,733 --> 00:01:54,467
Judging is harder than solving — so even a decent solver makes a weak judge

30
00:01:54,467 --> 00:01:58,500
Swap in a well-trained strong judge, and the story may change completely

31
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:02,367
Why does verification fall behind?

32
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:06,767
In one sentence: if the right answer is not among the candidates

33
00:02:06,767 --> 00:02:08,700
no amount of review will find it

34
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:13,000
Search widens the pool — fifty answers, some of them right

35
00:02:13,667 --> 00:02:15,533
Verification has only five

36
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:17,933
with one right answer, checking works

37
00:02:17,933 --> 00:02:20,133
without one, no review will help

38
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:23,133
Here, all five answers are wrong

39
00:02:23,767 --> 00:02:27,633
No matter how careful the judge is, no correct answer can come out

40
00:02:27,633 --> 00:02:30,333
that's a big part of why verification loses

41
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:33,033
How do you choose?

42
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:36,333
With fifty calls, two questions remain

43
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,533
First: how often is your model right?

44
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:43,167
At or below half, voting has no guarantee

45
00:02:43,267 --> 00:02:46,833
run a small probe and compare voting against answering once

46
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:51,933
In the gray zone — between fifty and sixty percent — probe first too

47
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:55,567
Above sixty percent, voting has a guarantee

48
00:02:56,233 --> 00:02:58,633
Second: how strong is your judge?

49
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:02,800
Weak — the model grading itself — then search wins

50
00:03:03,467 --> 00:03:06,533
Strong — a little verification is worth trying

51
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:15,733
Time to run the experiment. Fixed budget of fifty calls: search, or verify?

52
00:03:15,733 --> 00:03:17,900
We pick our model, and the setup is ready

53
00:03:18,733 --> 00:03:20,900
Eight configurations on the shared subset

54
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:24,367
four search budgets, four verify splits at the same budgets

55
00:03:25,233 --> 00:03:28,467
The analysis script rebuilds statistics from the recorded calls

56
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:32,500
records are the source of truth. Let's plot our results

57
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:35,200
Here is our budget-accuracy curve

58
00:03:35,367 --> 00:03:39,300
six problems move in large steps, so flat lines are expected

59
00:03:40,167 --> 00:03:43,400
The six-model p-spectrum — search beats weak verification

60
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,100
hollow over-budget markers stay below the lines

61
00:03:46,967 --> 00:03:50,400
We also check error dispersion: when the vote picks a wrong answer

62
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:52,933
do the losing candidates agree on the same mistake?

63
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:55,367
Finally, four questions

64
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:01,100
crossing points, catch-up budgets, your model's place in the p-spectrum, class collaboration

65
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:05,100
An optional experiment compares weak and strong verifiers

66
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,133
does a stronger judge change the winner?

67
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,500
Search beats verify under a weak verifier and moderate budget

68
00:04:12,367 --> 00:04:13,733
We'll point you to a paper

69
00:04:16,933 --> 00:04:19,733
This paper is where our budget curves come from

70
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:25,433
Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally, ICLR 2025

71
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:28,267
If you're interested in other related works

72
00:04:28,267 --> 00:04:30,867
check out a few more in our course PDF

73
00:04:33,267 --> 00:04:35,800
So — more answers, or more checking?

74
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:39,733
It depends on your model: weak judge, search wins

75
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:43,033
strong judge, a little verification goes a long way

76
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:47,300
Now it's your turn — find your own answer in our notebooks

